# Chapter 2 · Liberal Feminism

> Liberal feminism is the oldest and most widely accepted school. It says women are held back by **unfair laws, customs and lack of opportunity**, and that the answer is **equal rights within the existing system**. A 2023 MCQ asked what "the theory that men and women should be treated equally" is called. The answer: **liberal feminism**.

## 2.1  What "liberal" means here

"Liberal" here does not mean "modern" or "Western". It refers to **liberalism**, the political philosophy of the European Enlightenment (1600s–1700s). Liberalism holds that:

- every **individual** has **reason** — the ability to think and choose;
- every individual has **natural rights**, such as life, liberty and property;
- the state should treat citizens **equally** and protect their freedom.

Liberal feminists simply ask: **if these rights belong to "every individual", why not to women?**

::: define Liberal feminism | the school of equal rights
The view that women are as capable of **reason** as men, and that their disadvantage comes from **legal and customary barriers** and **unequal opportunities**. The remedy is **reform**: equal rights, equal education, equal access to work and public life.
-> Key words: individual, reason, rights, equal opportunity, reform, the public sphere.
:::

## 2.2  The first wave of liberal feminism

::: thinker Mary Wollstonecraft | English writer · *A Vindication of the Rights of Woman* (1792)
Wollstonecraft answered the French thinker **Jean-Jacques Rousseau**, whose book *Émile* (==1762==) said girls should be educated only to please and serve men. She argued that women appear weak and vain because they are **denied a rational education**. Give girls the same education as boys and they will become rational, virtuous citizens and better mothers.
-> Often called the founding text of liberal feminism.
:::

- **Olympe de Gouges** wrote the *Declaration of the Rights of Woman and of the Female Citizen* (==1791==) in answer to the French Revolution's *Declaration of the Rights of Man*. She was executed in ==1793==.
- At the **Seneca Falls Convention** (New York, ==1848==), **Elizabeth Cady Stanton** and **Lucretia Mott** presented the **Declaration of Sentiments**, modelled on the American Declaration of Independence: "We hold these truths to be self-evident: that all men and women are created equal."

::: thinker John Stuart Mill and Harriet Taylor Mill | *The Subjection of Women* (1869)
Mill argued that the legal subordination of women to men is **wrong in itself** and a **hindrance to human progress**; it should be replaced by **perfect equality**. He wrote that "what is now called the nature of women is an eminently artificial thing". His wife **Harriet Taylor Mill** had earlier written *The Enfranchisement of Women* (==1851==), and Mill said she shaped his ideas.
-> As a member of Parliament, Mill proposed in ==1867== that the word "man" in the Reform Bill be replaced by "person", to give women the vote. The proposal failed, but it launched the British suffrage campaign.
:::

The main goals of first-wave liberal feminism were the **vote**, **property rights for married women**, **access to education and the professions**, and **equal legal status**. New Zealand gave women the national vote in ==1893==; the United States in ==1920==; Britain partly in ==1918== and fully in ==1928==.

## 2.3  The second wave of liberal feminism

::: thinker Betty Friedan | American writer · *The Feminine Mystique* (1963)
Friedan described the unhappiness of educated American housewives in the 1950s — "**the problem that has no name**". The "feminine mystique" was the belief that women find complete fulfilment in being wives and mothers. Friedan urged women to seek education and careers.
-> In ==1966== she co-founded the **National Organization for Women (NOW)** and became its first president. The Feminine Mystique appeared in MCQs in both 2024 and 2025.
:::

Second-wave liberal feminists won major legal reforms, especially in the United States:

- the **Equal Pay Act** (==1963==);
- **Title VII** of the **Civil Rights Act** (==1964==), banning job discrimination on the basis of sex;
- campaigns for the (never ratified) **Equal Rights Amendment**;
- the **UN Convention on the Elimination of All Forms of Discrimination against Women (CEDAW)**, adopted in ==1979== — in spirit a liberal document of equal rights. Pakistan ratified it in ==1996==.

## 2.4  Later liberal feminism

- **Susan Moller Okin** (*Justice, Gender, and the Family*, ==1989==) argued that the family must itself be **just**. If the family is unequal, children learn injustice at home. This brought liberal ideas into the private sphere.
- **Martha Nussbaum** (*Women and Human Development*, ==2000==) developed the **capabilities approach** with **Amartya Sen**: justice means giving each person the real ability to do and be what they value — to be healthy, educated, safe, able to work and take part in politics. This version is widely used in development (Topic 4).
- **Zillah Eisenstein** (*The Radical Future of Liberal Feminism*, ==1981==) argued that the logic of equal rights, followed seriously, leads to deep social change.

## 2.5  The core ideas in one table

::: table | Liberal feminism on the four questions.
@widths 3.2,10.8
Question | Liberal answer
Cause | **Unequal laws, customs and opportunities**; prejudice and lack of education
Where | The **public sphere**: law, education, employment, politics
Remedy | **Reform**, not revolution: equal rights, equal pay, anti-discrimination laws, quotas, education
Method | Lobbying, lawsuits, legislation, elections, public campaigns
:::

::: fig flow | The liberal feminist chain of reasoning.
@per 3
Women have reason: they are rational individuals like men
So they have equal rights: to vote, own, learn, work
But law and custom deny them: exclusion and prejudice
So remove the barriers: reform law and education
Then women compete equally: equal opportunity
And society gains: more talent, more justice
:::

## 2.6  Strengths of liberal feminism

1. **Real achievements.** The vote, access to universities and professions, equal-pay and anti-discrimination laws — most of the legal gains women enjoy today came from liberal campaigns.
2. **Broad acceptance.** Its language of rights, fairness and merit persuades people who dislike more radical ideas.
3. **Works through institutions.** It uses courts, parliaments and constitutions, which makes it practical for policy.
4. **Fits international law.** CEDAW, the Sustainable Development Goals (Goal 5) and national constitutions speak its language.

## 2.7  Criticisms of liberal feminism

1. **It ignores structures.** Equal laws do not produce equal results when women still do most unpaid care. Radical and socialist feminists say it treats the symptoms, not the cause.
2. **The public/private divide.** Classic liberalism keeps the state out of the family — yet the family is where much inequality happens (housework, domestic violence).
3. **Equality as "sameness".** It asks women to become like men in a system built around men's lives, rather than changing the system. Difference feminists call this the **male norm**.
4. **Middle-class bias.** **bell hooks** criticised Friedan for writing about educated white housewives while ignoring poor and Black women who were already working.
5. **Individualism.** It focuses on individual choice and merit, underplaying group disadvantage.

::: fig compare | Liberal feminism: what it offers and what its critics say.
## What it offers
- Legal equality: the vote, property, education
- Anti-discrimination and equal-pay laws
- A language of rights that persuades widely
- Practical reform through institutions
## What critics say
- Laws change; structures stay
- Ignores the family and unpaid care
- Measures women against a male norm
- Speaks for middle-class women
:::

## 2.8  Liberal feminism in Pakistan

Liberal feminism is the most visible school in Pakistani law and policy, because the **Constitution of 1973** speaks its language.

::: law Constitution of Pakistan 1973 | the equality articles
- **Article 25:** all citizens are equal before law; "there shall be no discrimination on the basis of sex"; the state may make **special provision** for women and children.
- **Article 27:** no discrimination in public service on the basis of sex.
- **Article 34:** steps shall be taken to ensure **full participation of women** in all spheres of national life.
-> A liberal-feminist answer on Pakistan always starts from these three articles.
:::

- **Legal reforms:** the **Protection of Women (Criminal Laws Amendment) Act 2006**, the **Protection against Harassment of Women at the Workplace Act 2010**, the **honour-killing amendment of 2016**, the **Anti-Rape (Investigation and Trial) Act 2021**, and the **Islamabad Child Marriage Restraint Act 2025** (minimum age ==18==).
- **Political quotas:** ==60== reserved women's seats in the National Assembly (Topic 6).
- **Education:** campaigns for girls' schooling, symbolised internationally by **Malala Yousafzai** (Nobel Peace Prize, ==2014==).
- **Institutions:** the **National Commission on the Status of Women** (NCSW, ==2000==) and provincial women's commissions.

::: balance | Is liberal feminism enough for Pakistan?
**For:** it is constitutional, uses the language of rights that Pakistani courts understand, and has won real laws. **Against:** Pakistan has many good laws that are weakly enforced; poverty, custom and unpaid care keep most women from using their rights. Only ==24.4%== of working-age women are in the labour force (LFS 2024–25) despite legal equality in employment.
-> Liberal feminism supplies the **legal floor**. It needs economic and cultural strategies (Chapters 4 and 9) to turn rights on paper into rights in life.
:::
